  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mohit Gupta And Anr vs U. T. Of J&K Th. Sho Police Station on 12 March, 2026",
    "facts": "The petitioners have impugned FIR bearing No. 0237/2020 dated 14.10.2020 for commission of offences punishable under sections 272, 273, 328, 420, 120-B IPC, 7/16 of Food Safety Act and 3/7 of Essential Commodities Act registered with Police Station, Bari Brahmana, Samba. 2. It is stated that a bare perusal of the impugned FIR would reveal that the petitioners have been alleged to have committed offences under sections 272 and 273 IPC, but the respondent never had any report from the competent authority to suggest that there was adulteration in the food and while the case was under investigation, the Government Analyst, Jammu submitted the reports of the samples in question lifted pursuant to the impugned FIR and the said reports categorically declared Mohit Gupta And Anr vs U. T. Of J&K Th. Sho Police Station on 12 March, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/90317259/ 1 that the products lifted from the unit of the petitioners were of standard quality. 3. It is further submitted that no offence under Section 328 IPC is made out against the petitioners. It is contended that the said provision has been 2026:JKLHC-JMU:804 invoked solely with the intention to harass the petitioners and to stifle fair market competition, particularly as the petitioners have been established as formidable competitors for over 25 years. It is further submitted that the allegations, even if taken at their face value, do not satisfy the essential ingredients of the offences punishable under Section 420 IPC or under Sections 3/7 of the Essential Commodities Act. 4. Status report has been filed by the respondent, thereby stating the factual aspects of the case. Today, Mr. Romesh Chander, Investigating Officer, Police Station, Bari Brahmana, who is present in person in Court, has produced the CD file. It was alleged that the unit was illegally procuring readymade mustard and soybean oils from Punjab and repacking them under the brand name 'Zamindara Mustard Oil' in violation of SIDCO norms. Furthermore, it was stated that the owner was operating an additional unlicensed unit, 'Shree Ganesh Trading Wing,' on the same premises without obtaining the requisite license from the DIC. This second unit allegedly packed soybean oil under the brand 'Kamal Deep' without proper filtration or adherence to health and safety standards. Additionally, the FSSAI license for 'Ganpati Food Products' had reportedly expired, suggesting that the petitioners were operating with a common criminal intention and in a manner likely to spread dangerous diseases. 10. On receipt of this information, FIR bearing No. 237/2020 dated 14.10.2020 for commission of offences punishable under sections 272, 273, 328, 420, 120-B IPC, 7/16 of Food Safety Act and 3/7 of Essential Commodities Act was registered with Police Station, Bari Brahmana, Samba. Mohit Gupta And Anr vs U. T. Of J&K Th. Sho Police Station on 12 March, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/90317259/ 2 11. During investigation, Investigating Officer prepared the site plan and seized two oil tankers bearing registration Nos. PB04R 9497 and JK02AT- 5951 respectively and requested the Food Inspector to collect the samples of Soyabean and Mustard Oil from the premises of \"M/s Ganesh Trading Company\", Phase-II, Lane No. 4, SIDCO Complex, Bari Brahmana, Samba. Inspector, accordingly, collected the samples separately for chemical analyses in the laboratory. After the completion of requisite formalities, two tankers were released on the supurdnama and the premises of \"M/s Ganpati Trading Company\" was also de- sealed pursuant to order passed by learned Munsiff (JMIC) Samba. The Food Analyst opinion has been received and the results are reported to be negative.",
    "arguments": "5. Mr. Sachin Gupta, learned counsel for the petitioners has submitted that in view of the report of the Government Analyst, no offence is made out against the petitioners, and no FIR could have been registered against the petitioners for commission of offences under section 7/16 of the Food Safety Act. 6. Per contra, Mr. Vishal Bharti, learned Dy.AG representing respondent, has submitted that the appropriate orders in accordance with law may be passed.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "5. Mr. Sachin Gupta, learned counsel for the petitioners has submitted that in view of the report of the Government Analyst, no offence is made out against the petitioners, and no FIR could have been registered against the petitioners for commission of offences under section 7/16 of the Food Safety Act. 6. Per contra, Mr. Vishal Bharti, learned Dy.AG representing respondent, has submitted that the appropriate orders in accordance with law may be passed.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
